WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:04.040
When it comes to Marvel movies, there's a
tradition that what happens after the credits

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:07.609
roll can almost be as important as everything
that happens before.

00:00:07.609 --> 00:00:11.890
Spider-Man: Homecoming is no different, as
one of its two post-credits scenes hint at

00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:13.130
big things to come.

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:14.130
The other one?

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:16.120
Not so much...but it's still worth watching.

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:20.240
Let's get into some of the spoilers and explain
the post-credits scenes in Spider-Man: Homecoming.

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:22.100
Meet Mac Gargan

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:27.410
In the first and most significant post-credits
scene, the audience catches up with the defeated

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:31.460
Vulture, Adrian Toomes, walking through the
halls of prison after his showdown with Spider-Man.

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:36.640
There, he's approached by a scarred prisoner,
a criminal who was also on the Staten Island

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:38.980
Ferry as part of the Vulture's botched weapons
deal.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:44.100
That guy's Mac Gargan, and we get a good glimpse
at the scorpion tattoo on his neck.

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:49.400
Ladies and gentlemen, this is the Scorpion,
one of Spider-Man's oldest bad guys.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:51.240
Honor among thieves

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.340
Gargan asks Toomes about the rumor that he
knows Spider-Man's true identity.

00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:00.920
But the former Vulture denies everything,
paying Peter back for saving both his daughter's

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:01.940
life, and his own.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:06.770
Meanwhile, we know Gargan's got revenge on
his mind for the damage he took during the

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:07.340
Ferry fight.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.780
Setting up the Sinister Six

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.240
Gargan tells Toomes that he's got lots of
friends on the outside looking to go after

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:18.800
Spider-Man, which seems to be a big hint that
Spidey's bad guys will soon team up.

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:23.800
In the comics, they're known as the Sinister
Six, a group of villains with a rotating membership

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:24.300
over the years.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:28.490
With the Shocker still on the loose by the
end of Homecoming, it seems likely that we

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:32.130
already know at least two of the potential
members who will make up this evil gang of

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:32.640
jerks.

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:36.540
Seeing the Sinister Six in a Spidey sequel
wouldn't be too big of a surprise.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:41.060
It's well known that Sony intended to make
a spinoff Sinister Six movie following what

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:44.320
they'd hoped would be the success of The Amazing
Spider-Man 2.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.580
When that film underperformed, plans for the
movie were shelved.

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:51.440
That's when Sony worked out a deal with Marvel
to produce the 2017 reboot.

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:55.780
Now that Spidey's back in action with Sony
and Marvel hugging it out, it wouldn't be

00:01:55.799 --> 00:01:59.400
that shocking if the pre-production work that
was done on the Sinister Six film came out

00:01:59.400 --> 00:01:59.920
of the drawer.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:01.780
Oh, that Captain America thing

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:05.980
Viewers who stayed to the very end of the
credits were treated to one more scene — this

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:08.490
one featuring Captain America himself.

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:14.120
Standing against a white backdrop, Cap reminds
the audience of the virtue of patience...which

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:17.880
is a nice way of making fun of the fans who
stayed in the theater after the interminable

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:18.720
credits crawl.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:23.580
The joke hearkens back to the king of all
post-credits scenes: the one from Ferris Bueller's

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:24.080
Day Off.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:26.320
"You're still here?

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:30.520
It's over.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:33.660
Go home."

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:37.160
Does it have any relevance to the further
Marvel mythos?

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:38.460
Well, no.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:43.120
It might be the most pointless post-credits
scene in MCU history, considering that even

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:47.500
Guardians of the Galaxy's Howard the Duck
cameo technically featured the reintroduction

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:48.610
of the classic character.

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:51.340
"Why do you let it lick you?"

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:53.580
"Gross."

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:57.000
Anybody else feel like these post-credits
scenes might be a little overhyped at this

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:57.500
point?

00:02:58.740 --> 00:02:59.280
"It's over.

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:01.340
Go home."

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:02.380
Thanks for watching!

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:04.760
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:07.120
Plus check out all this other cool stuff we
know you'll love too.

